1. Set Up zelavis as an Open-Source OrganizationTo make zelavis an "organization" (not just a personal repo), structure it for collaboration and longevity. This positions it as a community-owned project.Create a GitHub Organization: Go to github.com/new/organization and set up zelavis as an org (free tier is fine to start).  
   Transfer your monorepo (e.g., the zelavis repo) to this org.  
   Invite early contributors as members (start with friends or interested devs from your network).  
   Add an org README/profile: Include your mission ("Universal JS libraries — community-driven, runtime-agnostic"), code of conduct (use Contributor Covenant template), contribution guidelines (e.g., "We welcome PRs for new packages, bug fixes, docs"), and a link to zelavis.dev.

License Everything Properly: Use MIT or Apache 2.0 for all packages — permissive licenses encourage adoption and community contributions.  
Add LICENSE files to the monorepo root and each package.

Make It Truly Open: Set up issues/templates for bugs, features, discussions.  
Enable GitHub Discussions for community Q&A/forums (better than issues for non-code talk).  
Use GitHub Projects or Actions for transparent roadmaps/workflows.

This setup makes zelavis feel like a collective effort from day one, attracting contributors who see it as "ours" not "yours."2. Build a Great Community Around zelavisCommunity is key to sustainability — it drives contributions, adoption, and donations. Aim for organic growth while being proactive.Core Strategies: Share Your Vision Early: Write a "Why zelavis?" blog post on zelavis.dev (or Medium/X/Dev.to) explaining the universal libs philosophy, how it differs from UnJS, and your donation-sustained model. Emphasize "community-first" — invite people to join discussions, propose packages, or co-maintain.  
Engage on Social/Forums: Post on X (use #OpenSource #JavaScript #JSlibs) about milestones (e.g., "Just shipped @zelavis
/core v1.0! Who's building with it?").  
Share on Reddit (r/javascript, r/opensource, r/webdev) and Discord servers (JS-focused like The JS Foundation or create a zelavis Discord).  
Use X Semantic Search or Keyword Search (if needed) to find related conversations and join them authentically.

Host Events/Activities: Run "hackathons" or "contribathons" on GitHub (e.g., label easy issues as "good first issue").  
Weekly X Spaces or YouTube streams: "Building with zelavis — Q&A and demos."  
Create a newsletter (via Buttondown or Substack) for updates, contributor spotlights, and calls for donations.

Contributor Onboarding: Make CONTRIBUTING.md welcoming: Step-by-step setup (pnpm install, build scripts), code style (ESLint/Prettier), and testing (Vitest/Jest).  
Recognize contributors: Shoutouts in release notes, "Hall of Fame" on the site, or GitHub badges.

Metrics to Track Growth: GitHub stars/forks/watchers.  
npm downloads per package.  
Community size: Discord members, X followers, newsletter subs.

